Token parsing: for each line, split on whitespace, lowercase each token.

Definition handling: a token `:` starts a definition; the next token is the name; then tokens until `;` are the body. A definition may span multiple lines. The name must not be a number. If the name is a number, error "illegal operation".

At definition time, resolve the body tokens: for each token, if it's a number → push action; otherwise look up in dict → reference action. If not found, error "undefined operation"? Hmm — in real Forth, defining a word that references an undefined word is an error at definition time. The tests don't cover this case, but it's reasonable. Actually, wait — is it? Let me think: the test "errors when running a non-existent word" is about execution. For definitions referencing undefined words, erroring at definition time is standard Forth behavior. Let's do that.

